{"sequence":16,"timestamp":"2026-03-30T09:37:41.939917914Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-1-974e021f","body":"shrimp.rs: `gh_mineral_factor` is now double-counted in spawning. It was added to `reproductive_readiness_factors` (line 1478, the new 8th factor feeding the EMA readiness index), but `f_mineral` is still multiplied directly in `spawn_rate` at line 731 (`base_spawn_rate * spawn_readiness * f_mineral`). This means poor mineral conditions have a squared suppressive effect on spawning — once through the smoothed EMA, once through the direct multiplier. Either remove `minerals` from `reproductive_readiness_factors` (keeping only the direct path, matching pre-change behavior), or remove the direct `f_mineral` from `spawn_rate` (letting the EMA handle it). The same double-counting also applies to hatch rate (line 890) where `f_mineral` appears directly while the EMA readiness is not used — but hatch rate is a separate path so only spawning is affected.","dedup_key":"a605f24bd7c944de695ed0b74c3dee31a6b2729f7f024a21f5e05f902c26ff0e","run_id":"run-20260330083637","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
{"sequence":17,"timestamp":"2026-03-30T09:37:41.949368773Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-1-6652b2b9","body":"e2e_habitat_geometry_probes.rs probe_light_depth: the summary shows both shallow and deep tanks have *negative* plant growth (-0.40g and -0.71g respectively). The assertion `growth_shallow > growth_deep` passes only because the shallow tank loses less biomass. The doc comment claims the probe validates 'slower plant growth' in deep water and 'more plant biomass growth' in shallow water, but neither tank actually *grows* plants. This undermines the mechanistic narrative — the probe demonstrates light-driven differential decline rather than the claimed light-driven growth difference. Consider adjusting initial conditions (more nutrients, longer photoperiod, or higher light intensity) so the shallow tank achieves net positive growth, making the directional comparison genuinely demonstrate the Beer-Lambert mechanism.","dedup_key":"b383e48dc168fb7a2b5b320d005c8be20244e278d921871d3a555c1d466b5483","run_id":"run-20260330083637","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
{"sequence":18,"timestamp":"2026-03-30T09:37:41.958769517Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-1-cb2f741c","body":"e2e_habitat_geometry_probes.rs probe_equipment_scaling: `concentration_tolerance` is set to 0.55 (55%), meaning the 2× tank can have concentrations 55% divergent from the 1× tank and still pass. The actual divergence is ~32% for peak TAN and ~39% for DO range. The acceptance criteria state 'per-liter concentrations and qualitative outcomes stay within envelope across both sizes' — 55% tolerance is very permissive for a scaling-equivalence test. Consider whether a tighter tolerance (e.g., 35-40%) would better validate the scaling invariant while still accommodating legitimate non-linear effects.","dedup_key":"3da8dbcd2cb9ee36006ae5e67130f8f69231942534d731dfa766ad79186e62ff","run_id":"run-20260330083637","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
